# React Frontend Conventions

React 19, functional components only, TypeScript strict. Follows `CLAUDE-FRONTEND.md` for everything not covered here; the framework file (`CLAUDE-FRONTEND-NEXT.md` or `CLAUDE-FRONTEND-VITE.md`) covers routing and entry points.

## Files

- Components `PascalCase.tsx`; hooks `camelCase.ts` with a `use` prefix; context providers `PascalCaseProvider.tsx`.
- Hooks and providers live in `state/`. No separate `hooks/` directory.

## Component shape

```tsx
import { useCallback, useState } from 'react';
import type { FormEvent } from 'react';
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { fetchMessages } from '@/api/fetchMessages';
import styles from './ChatBox.module.scss';

interface ChatBoxProps {
    tripId: string;
    onSend: (message: string) => void;
}

export default function ChatBox({ tripId, onSend }: ChatBoxProps) {
    // state, handlers
    return <div className={styles.chatBox} data-test-id="chat-box">{/* ... */}</div>;
}

ChatBox.displayName = 'ChatBox';
```

- Default exports for components and pages; named exports for hooks and services.
- Props interface `{ComponentName}Props`, above the component in the same file. Destructure props in the signature.
- Plain function declarations; no `React.FC`, no class components.
- Wrap handlers and async functions passed as props in `useCallback`.
- Every component and page sets `displayName` and a kebab-case `data-test-id`.
- No inline styles; use SCSS modules. JSX attribute quotes are double (`jsxSingleQuote: false`).
- Import group 1 is React plus the framework's own packages; style imports come last.

## State

- TanStack Query for all server state, through `useQuery` and `useMutation` wrapping the `api/` functions. Never `useEffect` + `fetch`. `onError` routes to the toast.
- React Context for auth and app-wide concerns; `useState` for local UI state; `useRef` for DOM refs and stable references (EventSource, timers).
- No Redux, Zustand or other state libraries.
- Radix UI for dialogs, toasts, menus and other headless primitives, styled with SCSS modules. Never put `role="button"` on a non-interactive element; use the primitive or a native `<button>`.

## Lint and tests

- ESLint: `react-hooks/rules-of-hooks` as error, `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` as warn, `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y` recommended; `eslint-config-next` in Next projects, `eslint-plugin-react-refresh` in Vite projects.
- Component tests: Vitest with jsdom, React Testing Library, `jest-dom` and `user-event`. Query by role and accessible name, not class names.
- Storybook stories for shared `components/ui/*`, with visual-regression snapshots in the Playwright `visual-regression` project.
